# Copy to `.env` and adjust. Everything here is baked into the JS bundle at
# build time (Expo's EXPO_PUBLIC_* convention), so it must not hold secrets —
# the Keycloak client is a public OIDC client with no secret.
#
# For local dev against the stack in skateboard-infrastructure you can usually
# leave all of these unset: the BFF URL is derived from the host Metro served
# this bundle from, which is correct on device, emulator, simulator and web
# alike. See src/core/config/env.ts.

# Override when the BFF is not on the dev machine (a deployed environment), or
# to pin a host explicitly. Must be reachable *from the device*, not just from
# your machine — see the note at the bottom.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_BFF_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com

# Must be byte-identical to APP_SECURITY_OAUTH2_ISSUER_URI on skateboard-
# ui-backend, skateboard-podcast-be and skateboard-app-config-be: it becomes
# the `iss` claim on every token, and each service rejects a mismatch. Changing
# it here alone gets you a successful login followed by 401s on every call.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_KEYCLOAK_ISSUER=http://localhost:8180/realms/skateboard-podcast
# EXPO_PUBLIC_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID=skateboard-podcast-fe

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Testing on a physical device or Android emulator
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One variable lives outside this file and still has to be set by hand.
#
# The login background and app logo are served as presigned S3 URLs minted by
# skateboard-app-config-be, whose host comes from that service's
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL (default http://localhost:9000, the MinIO container). The
# device receives that host verbatim, so with the default it asks *itself* for
# the images and renders nothing — while the web build, running on the dev
# machine where `localhost` is correct, looks perfectly fine.
#
# It cannot be rewritten on the client: SigV4 signs the Host header, so
# changing the hostname invalidates the signature and the store returns 403.
# Start the backend with a host the device can reach:
#
#   AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=http://192.168.1.5:9000   # `ipconfig getifaddr en0`
#
# 10.0.2.2 works in place of the LAN IP on an Android emulator.
